WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.104
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.104 --> 00:00:11.318
At the minimum level, a value chain
consists of a supplier, consists of

00:00:11.318 --> 00:00:15.590
component suppliers, sub-assemblies, final
assembly, manufacturing, distributors,

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:18.934
retailers, wholesalers and, ultimately,
would be the end consumers.

00:00:18.934 --> 00:00:23.195
And this whole chain of network that

00:00:23.195 --> 00:00:28.594
are engaged in the production of products
and

00:00:28.594 --> 00:00:33.864
services, that's the basic value chain.

00:00:33.864 --> 00:00:38.178
But now, increasingly, value chain could
include the government because they

00:00:38.178 --> 00:00:41.414
could affect and they could limit, they
could restrict, or

00:00:41.414 --> 00:00:46.550
they could support your, your whole, whole
delivery of products and services.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:52.670
It could include even NGOs because they
are also a stakeholder.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:57.170
They could also include logistics
providers, third party providers.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:02.561
So, I call that the complete value chain
or the ecosystem.

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:06.044
[MUSIC]

00:01:06.044 --> 00:01:10.340
So today, very few companies are
completely vertically integrated, meaning,

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.280
that you make everything.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.050
You make all the components yourself, you
make all the sub-assemblies yourself, and

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.510
you distribute yourself.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.710
Everyone depend on somebody else.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:24.020
So you depend on your suppliers, you
depend on your outlet, you dis,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.950
depend on your distributor, you depend on
some third party as a channel.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:34.410
And if you're not able to integrate that
value chain well synchronized,

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.430
then you could be very efficient.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.440
You could be very successful.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:43.130
But if your value chain partners cannot
fulfill their job,

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:48.550
your products may not be successful or
your services cannot

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:53.950
reach to the customers in your intended
fashion and expectation.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.698
So we're all interdependent, and in order
for

00:01:56.698 --> 00:02:00.901
a company to be successful, the whole
value chain has to be successful.

00:02:00.901 --> 00:02:04.361
[MUSIC]

00:02:04.361 --> 00:02:07.763
Most of the entrepreneurs in the
developing economies recognize that they

00:02:07.763 --> 00:02:10.949
have huge constraints because the
government may not be supportive,

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:11.921
their roads are bad,

00:02:11.921 --> 00:02:15.650
the infrastructures are not dependable,
their suppliers are not on time.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:17.620
So they all recognize these challenges.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:21.990
Also, what I would tell these
entrepreneurs is that

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.130
you can overcome this.

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:28.620
These barriers are not insurmountable.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:34.450
And you overcome it by, sometimes,
redefining what your roles are, creating

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:40.660
the right information linkages, creating
the right relationships, sharing, sharing,

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.870
collaborating, and those challenges and
constraints can be overcome.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:49.700
And once they are overcome, there could be
new business models and new products and

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.900
services that you have not even thought of
today.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:57.414
And so that's why I, my simple advice is,
challenges are great but

00:02:57.414 --> 00:03:02.559
they can be overcome by the right
innovations and approaches.

00:03:02.559 --> 00:03:05.925
[MUSIC]

00:03:05.925 --> 00:03:13.640
Innovations that could require you to
rethink the way you deliver your products.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.556
Rethink about the incentives that you give
to suppliers.

00:03:17.556 --> 00:03:21.321
So sometimes those barriers are because
the suppliers do not trust you or

00:03:21.321 --> 00:03:24.346
because the suppliers do not have the
right information or

00:03:24.346 --> 00:03:27.325
because the suppliers have their own
constraints.

00:03:27.325 --> 00:03:30.555
And so helping them by collaborating with
them,

00:03:30.555 --> 00:03:36.510
you overcome their barriers, which, in
turn unblock your own bottleneck.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:41.111
So I would say that, by going out having
the right information shared,

00:03:41.111 --> 00:03:46.482
having the right collaborations in place,
having the right incentives in place,

00:03:46.482 --> 00:03:47.646
can go a long way.

00:03:47.646 --> 00:03:51.105
[MUSIC]

00:03:51.105 --> 00:03:56.023
When we went to Ghana, when we went to
these developing economies, oftentimes

00:03:56.023 --> 00:04:00.738
the entrepreneurs because of the
experience and because of the limitations

00:04:00.738 --> 00:04:05.570
that they have lived with all their lives,
they only think of small markets.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:09.505
They only think of, okay, what can I do to
create this service so that I

00:04:09.505 --> 00:04:14.279
can serve my village, so that I can serve
my town, my own region, my own geography?

00:04:14.279 --> 00:04:19.076
But, in fact, if they understand the
complete value chain well,

00:04:19.076 --> 00:04:20.670
they can export.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.300
They can serve other countries.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.310
They can expand what they're doing.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:28.870
I'll give you an example of the farmers
that I work with in Ghana.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:33.170
These are shea butter Ghana's farmers, the
women farmers.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:37.750
So they roll shea butter, which are nuts.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:42.892
But it turns out that the biggest value
add is if they can also learn how

00:04:42.892 --> 00:04:49.210
to process the nuts so that they become
ingredients for cosmetic companies.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:54.130
And that is a much higher value add than
just purely growing the nuts,

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:59.850
using the nuts for food consumption, their
own people consuming the nuts.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:05.184
And so this is really about a way of
understanding what role you play

00:05:05.184 --> 00:05:11.565
in the big value chain, and can you add
more value by doing more than what you're

00:05:11.565 --> 00:05:17.106
doing today and by expanding the
geographic span that you can cover.

00:05:17.106 --> 00:05:21.772
[MUSIC]

00:05:21.772 --> 00:05:27.473
Such innovations are not just for the sake
of helping the developed economies so that

00:05:27.473 --> 00:05:33.260
they would create values for themselves
and, and make people live better there.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:38.860
I would have to say that if you are a
multinational today, large corporations,

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:43.510
we also, our own students, MBA students,
our own alumni,

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:48.610
we also better understand those economies
better.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:53.990
We also better learn how to leverage the
entrepreneurial power

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:56.080
of those economies better.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.340
We also need to leverage the innovations
that could come from so called bottom

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:08.660
of the pyramid because those innovations
can also be incorporated into these if,

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:14.160
even if you're a big corporation, because
big corporations oftentime have to expand.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.470
And you may, one day, go to those
economies.

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:19.690
Those economies could be your market.

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:22.900
This is like about 30 years ago, 25 years
ago.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:27.190
People would think of China as a
developing economy.

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:31.110
And you would only use China as a place
where you

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:36.290
can have cheap labors source to build your
products, to manufacture your products.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:40.460
But today, China is no longer a developing
economy,

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.440
even though pockets of China are still
developing.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.280
But China is a market.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:46.750
China is a big market.

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:48.760
China is a source of innovation.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:51.950
Same thing could happen in different parts
of Africa,

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:55.690
in different parts of southeast Asia, in
different parts of South America.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:57.170
So I would say that,

00:06:57.170 --> 00:07:02.290
why we go out there with the intent to
help the entrepreneurs in the region.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:07.148
But I think, regardless of whether you are
looking at the interest of

00:07:07.148 --> 00:07:11.925
big corporations in developed economies,
this kind of value chain

00:07:11.925 --> 00:07:17.396
innovations are crucial for the overall
economic development of everyone.

00:07:17.396 --> 00:07:27.224
[MUSIC]

